IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Row il-77, TARGETED: the Population Registry Law, 5725-1965 ("the Law"). Draft, target compiler main. No domain expert has read this against the source.
-- THIS ROW DOES NOT ENCODE THE LAW. It records two items (I1 and I2), each a reference in the Law as typed values, and tests them.
-- SEAT: a reader who relies on a registry copy or certificate (item I1), and the person who gives the notice of a birth outside an institution (item I2).
-- A row of references asks that individual no question: there is no input to ask for, and no @export.
-- NOUNS: an instrument, a place in a law, a paragraph label, an instruction an instrument prints, a text as an instrument printed it, a printed period,
-- a commencement as the page prints it, a pointer to a range of paragraphs, a pointer to another law, an order that fixes a limit, an item, and the questions asked of them.
-- No reference is stated here; they are in pr77-cross-references.l4.
--
-- Every item is recorded with two limbs, as in the other targeted rows:
--   limb (a): the target is repealed, missing or says something else, read from an official instrument;
--   limb (b): the citing words still read so in the latest official instrument read.
-- Subsection letters and the letters of paragraph labels are written with Latin names for the Hebrew letters the Law uses: (א) is Alef, (ב) Bet, (ג) Gimel, (ד) Dalet.
-- THE NAMES BELOW ARE MINE, not the Law's. A date typed as an issue date is the date the footer of the issue prints; it is NOT a commencement date.
-- A commencement is typed separately, and only as the page prints it: a calendar date where the page prints one, a period where it prints a period. No date is computed from a period.

§ `Nouns`

DECLARE `A way a fact was read` IS ONE OF
    `official page image`
    `official page image, with the consolidation agreeing (CONSOLIDATION-ONLY)`
    `official page images, completeness CONSOLIDATION-ONLY`
    `printed as a period, no calendar date computed`
    `not established from a page`
    `CONSOLIDATION-ONLY`
    `not checked`

DECLARE `A Hebrew letter` IS ONE OF
    Alef
    Bet
    Gimel
    Dalet

-- The laws the row names. The titles are working translations.
DECLARE `A law` IS ONE OF
    `the Population Registry Law`
    `the Genetic Information Law`
    `the Interpretation Law`

DECLARE `A series` IS ONE OF
    `Sefer HaChukim`
    `Kovetz HaTakanot`

-- An issue of Sefer HaChukim or of Kovetz HaTakanot. The issue date is the one the footer prints.
-- For the 1965 issue the footer's last digit of the issue number is not certain in the scan (NOTES.md, section 3), so the flag is FALSE there.
DECLARE `An instrument` HAS
    `its series` IS A `A series`
    `its issue number` IS A NUMBER
    `the last digit of its issue number is certain in the scan` IS A BOOLEAN
    `its issue date` IS A DATE
    `its working title` IS A STRING

-- The label of a paragraph: a number, and a letter where the label has one, as in (11א).
DECLARE `A paragraph label` HAS
    `its number` IS A NUMBER
    `its letter` IS A MAYBE `A Hebrew letter`

-- A place in a law: a section (with a letter where it has one, as in 28א), optionally a subsection, optionally a paragraph of it.
-- A place that is a definition carries the defined term the page prints in marks.
DECLARE `A place` HAS
    `its law` IS A `A law`
    `its section` IS A NUMBER
    `its section letter` IS A MAYBE `A Hebrew letter`
    `its subsection` IS A MAYBE `A Hebrew letter`
    `its paragraph` IS A MAYBE `A paragraph label`
    `the term it defines` IS A MAYBE STRING

DECLARE `A unit of time` IS ONE OF
    Days
    Months

-- A period as the page prints it, in its own unit.
DECLARE `A printed period` HAS
    `its count` IS A NUMBER
    `its unit` IS A `A unit of time`

-- A commencement as read. The date is typed only where the page prints a calendar date; the period only where the page prints a period; neither is computed from the other.
DECLARE `A commencement as read` HAS
    `the instrument` IS AN `An instrument`
    `the date the page prints` IS A MAYBE DATE
    `the period the page prints` IS A MAYBE `A printed period`
    `how it was read` IS A `A way a fact was read`

DECLARE `A kind of instruction` IS ONE OF
    Insert
    `Mark and add after`
    `Replace definition`
    `Marked as void`

-- An instruction an instrument prints about a law.
-- `the words it names` are the words the instruction itself prints as the ones it concerns, `the words it puts` those it prints as put in; both are copied from the ext: line above the value.
-- `the place of the instruction in its instrument` is a gloss in English, for example "section 8".
-- For a "Marked as void" instruction the page prints the one word בטל for the subsection; the name is a working translation and not a claim about legal effect.
DECLARE `An instruction` HAS
    `the instrument` IS AN `An instrument`
    `the place of the instruction in its instrument` IS A STRING
    `the printed page it is on` IS A NUMBER
    `the kind` IS A `A kind of instruction`
    `the place it names` IS A `A place`
    `it comes after the label` IS A MAYBE `A paragraph label`
    `the label it inserts` IS A MAYBE `A paragraph label`
    `the words it names` IS A LIST OF STRING
    `the words it puts` IS A LIST OF STRING
    `the commencement as read` IS A `A commencement as read`
    `a page image of it is deposited` IS A BOOLEAN

-- A text as an instrument printed it: a paragraph, a subsection, a section or a definition.
DECLARE `A printed text` HAS
    `the instrument that printed it` IS AN `An instrument`
    `the printed page it is on` IS A NUMBER
    `the place it is printed at` IS A `A place`
    `its words` IS A STRING

-- I1: a pointer inside a section to a range of paragraphs of a subsection, written as two end labels.
DECLARE `A range pointer` HAS
    `the printed text that holds it` IS A `A printed text`
    `the pointer's words` IS A STRING
    `the first label` IS A `A paragraph label`
    `the last label` IS A `A paragraph label`
    `the subsection the range is of` IS A `A place`

-- I2: the two pointers a paragraph carries to another law: to a subsection, and to a defined term of that law.
DECLARE `A pointer to another law` HAS
    `the printed text that holds it` IS A `A printed text`
    `the pointer to a subsection, in its words` IS A STRING
    `the place that pointer names` IS A `A place`
    `the pointer to a defined term, in its words` IS A STRING

-- A section of an instrument that prints a period, an earlier notice that can end it, and an extension by order, as it prints them.
DECLARE `A commencement with a notice and an extension` HAS
    `the commencement as read` IS A `A commencement as read`
    `the printed page it is on` IS A NUMBER
    `an earlier notice in Reshumot can end the period` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the longest extension by order the page prints` IS A `A printed period`

-- An order that sets a later limit than the one a commencement section prints. The limit is typed as a limit, and not as a commencement date.
DECLARE `An order that fixes a limit` HAS
    `the instrument` IS AN `An instrument`
    `the printed page it is on` IS A NUMBER
    `the limit the page prints` IS A DATE
    `an earlier notice in Reshumot can end it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it names a section of the Population Registry Law` IS A BOOLEAN

-- An item of the row, with its two limbs.
DECLARE `An item` HAS
    `the item` IS A STRING
    `the citing words are in` IS A STRING
    `limb (a) holds` IS A BOOLEAN
    `limb (a) was read` IS A `A way a fact was read`
    `limb (b) holds` IS A BOOLEAN
    `limb (b) was read` IS A `A way a fact was read`
    `the instruments and pages read` IS A STRING

-- ===== QUESTIONS ASKED OF THE VALUES =====

GIVEN t IS A `A printed text`, w IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the printed text contains the words` MEANS CONTAINS (t's `its words`) w

GIVEN a IS AN `An instrument`, b IS AN `An instrument`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the first instrument was issued after the second` MEANS a's `its issue date` > b's `its issue date`

GIVEN a IS AN `An instrument`, b IS AN `An instrument`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the two instruments are two different issues` MEANS NOT ((a's `its series` EQUALS b's `its series`) AND (a's `its issue number` EQUALS b's `its issue number`))

GIVEN c IS A `A commencement as read`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the commencement is established from a page` MEANS NOT (c's `how it was read` EQUALS `not established from a page`)

-- ===== I1: THE ORDER OF THE LABELS OF A SUBSECTION =====
-- A list of labels always comes last among the parameters of a function (the parser reads "LIST OF T, name IS" as a longer type).

-- The labels of a subsection in an order, as a record, for the functions that take two lists.
DECLARE `An order of labels` HAS
    `its labels` IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`

-- The list from the first occurrence of the label to the end; EMPTY if the label is not in the list.
GIVEN lo IS A `A paragraph label`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels from` MEANS
    CONSIDER ls
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY ys THEN IF y EQUALS lo THEN ls ELSE `the labels from` lo ys

-- The list up to and including the first occurrence of the label; the whole list if the label is not in it.
GIVEN hi IS A `A paragraph label`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels up to and including` MEANS
    CONSIDER ls
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY ys THEN IF y EQUALS hi THEN LIST y ELSE y FOLLOWED BY (`the labels up to and including` hi ys)

-- The labels that lie from the label lo to the label hi, both ends included, in the order of the list.
GIVEN lo IS A `A paragraph label`, hi IS A `A paragraph label`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels from a to b` MEANS `the labels up to and including` hi (`the labels from` lo ls)

GIVEN lo IS A `A paragraph label`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels after` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the labels from` lo ls
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY ys THEN ys

GIVEN lo IS A `A paragraph label`, hi IS A `A paragraph label`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the first label comes before the second in the list` MEANS elem hi (`the labels after` lo ls)

-- The list after one instruction of kind Insert: the label it inserts goes directly after the label it names as the one it comes after.
-- An instruction that names no label, or no label to come after, leaves the list as it was.
GIVEN i IS AN `An instruction`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels after one insertion` MEANS
    CONSIDER i's `the label it inserts`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN ls
    WHEN JUST new THEN
        CONSIDER i's `it comes after the label`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN ls
        WHEN JUST prior THEN `the labels with the label put after` new prior ls

GIVEN new IS A `A paragraph label`, prior IS A `A paragraph label`, ls IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels with the label put after` MEANS
    CONSIDER ls
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY ys THEN
        IF y EQUALS prior
        THEN y FOLLOWED BY (new FOLLOWED BY ys)
        ELSE y FOLLOWED BY (`the labels with the label put after` new prior ys)

-- The list after a list of instructions, applied in the order given (the order of their issue dates).
GIVEN o IS AN `An order of labels`, is IS A LIST OF `An instruction`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
`the labels after the insertions` MEANS
    CONSIDER is
    WHEN EMPTY THEN o's `its labels`
    WHEN i FOLLOWED BY rest THEN `the labels after the insertions` (`An order of labels` WITH `its labels` IS `the labels after one insertion` i (o's `its labels`)) rest

-- Only the instructions whose commencement is established from a page.
GIVEN is IS A LIST OF `An instruction`
GIVETH A LIST OF `An instruction`
`the instructions whose commencement is established` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN i YIELD `the commencement is established from a page` (i's `the commencement as read`)) is

-- I1 limb (a) as the red assertion states it: every label that lies from the label lo to the label hi in the current list is a label the printed order holds from lo to hi.
GIVEN printed IS AN `An order of labels`, lo IS A `A paragraph label`, hi IS A `A paragraph label`, current IS A LIST OF `A paragraph label`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`every label from a to b is a label the printed list has there` MEANS
    all (GIVEN x YIELD elem x (`the labels from a to b` lo hi (printed's `its labels`))) (`the labels from a to b` lo hi current)

-- ===== I2: IS A PLACE IN THE TEXT OF A LAW AS THE INSTRUCTIONS LEFT IT =====

-- The place is the one the original text prints, and no instruction in the list, issued after the instrument that printed the original, names the place and marks it void.
GIVEN p IS A `A place`, orig IS A `A printed text`, is IS A LIST OF `An instruction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the place is in the text as the instructions in the list left it` MEANS
    (orig's `the place it is printed at` EQUALS p)
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN i YIELD ((i's `the place it names` EQUALS p) AND (i's `the kind` EQUALS `Marked as void`) AND ((orig's `the instrument that printed it`)'s `its issue date` < (i's `the instrument`)'s `its issue date`))) is)

GIVEN i IS AN `An instruction`, w IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the instruction names the words` MEANS elem w (i's `the words it names`)

GIVEN c IS AN `An item`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`both limbs hold` MEANS c's `limb (a) holds` AND c's `limb (b) holds`
